<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph-Louis Lagrange</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1736">1736</a>–<a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Giuseppe Luigi Lagrangia, Giuseppe Luigi Lagrange</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph-Louis Lagrange was an Italian and naturalized French mathematician, physicist and astronomer, born Giuseppe Luigi Lagrangia and educated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-turin">University of Turin</a>. In 1766 he succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler">Leonhard Euler</a> as director of mathematics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-academy-of-sciences">Prussian Academy of Sciences</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he wrote the Mécanique analytique (1788), which turned Newtonian mechanics into a branch of analysis. He was one of the creators of the calculus of variations, proved that every natural number is a sum of four squares and in 1772 found the solutions now known as Lagrangian points. Moving to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1787, he became the first professor of analysis at the École Polytechnique in 1794 and died in Paris in 1813.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-louis-lagrange.jpg" alt="Joseph-Louis Lagrange" width="330" height="369" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lagrange_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
